{"version" 2 "boxes" [{"id" "chart_expect" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "4 - An assertion you can watch fail" "_tombstone" false "frame" #afield/rect [634.2 226.0 534.2 136.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "chart_expect_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\n`Chart.expect` is `Table.expect`'s counterpart, and it DRAWS the claim rather than\nonly checking it: the accepted range becomes a band, the bound it names is ruled,\nthe data outside recolours, and the footnote counts them. An unbounded side draws no\nedge — that is the visual difference between \"at most 1e-6\" and \"0 to 1e-6\".\n*/\nval iterations = (0..40).toList()\nval residual = iterations.map { i ->\n    val base = exp(-i * 0.22) * 1.5e-6\n    if (i == 11 || i == 23) base + 2.4e-6 else base // two real overshoots\n}\n\nval c = Chart(\n    title = \"residual per iteration\",\n    xLabel = \"iteration\",\n    yLabel = \"|r|\",\n    note = \"Adam, lr 0.05\",\n).line(\"residual\", iterations, residual)\n    .points(\"residual\", iterations, residual)\n    .expect(max = 1e-6, because = \"converged below 1e-6\")\n\nshowChart(c)\n\n// And `deviations` hands the same list back, so a test asserts on the claim the figure\n// makes rather than on its markup. This is the whole reason the primitive earns its keep.\nfacts(c.deviations.map { \"iteration ${it.index}\" to it.y })\n"}}} {"id" "chart_dataframe" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "2 - A DataFrame charts itself" "_tombstone" false "frame" #afield/rect [634.2 60.0 463.9 136.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "chart_dataframe_code_.kt" "inline" "import org.jetbrains.kotlinx.dataframe.api.*\n\n/*\nA frame already renders as a table — that is the right compact form, because a frame\nis rows and columns first. This is the other question a reader has about a numeric\ncolumn: what shape is it.\n*/\nval runs = dataFrameOf(\"iteration\", \"residual\", \"step\")(\n    0,\n    1.00,\n    0.50,\n    1,\n    0.62,\n    0.44,\n    2,\n    0.31,\n    0.39,\n    3,\n    0.18,\n    0.35,\n    4,\n    0.09,\n    0.31,\n    5,\n    0.05,\n    0.28,\n    6,\n    0.02,\n    0.25,\n)\n\n// No column named: the first numeric column becomes the x axis and the rest become\n// series, each with a pickable points mark in the line's own colour.\nshowChart(runs, title = \"a frame, charting its own columns\")\n\n// ...and the last expression is still the frame, so the table is right underneath it.\nruns\n"}}} {"id" "chart_one_call" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "1 - A chart, in one call" "_tombstone" false "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 60.0 405.3 136.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "chart_one_call_code_.kt" "inline" "// One call. `showChart` builds it, puts it on screen, and hands it back so you can\n// keep asking it questions — the same shape as `showTable` and `showSignals`.\nval t = (0..240).map { it / 240.0 * 6.2831853 }\nval chart = showChart(\n    \"damped sine\" to t.map { exp(-it * 0.25) * sin(it * 2.0) },\n    \"envelope\" to t.map { exp(-it * 0.25) },\n    x = t,\n    title = \"a chart, in one call\",\n    xLabel = \"t (radians)\",\n    yLabel = \"amplitude\",\n)\n\n// Query readable axis ticks and plot bounds when placing your own annotations.\nprintln(chart)\nprintln(\"y ticks: \" + niceTicks(-1.0, 1.0, 5))\nprintln(\"plot frame: \" + chart.plotRect())\n"}}} {"id" "chart_pick" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "3 - Hover a bar, click a datum" "_tombstone" false "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 226.0 475.6 136.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "chart_pick_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\nBar and point marks carry labels, hover colors and click handlers.\nThese make the chart interactive through the normal lines renderer.\n\nHover a bar: it lights up. Click one: this handler runs, here, in this box.\n*/\nval c =\n    Chart(title = \"hover a bar, then click one\", yLabel = \"gates\")\n        .bars(\"cost\", [\"xor\", \"maj3\", \"adder\", \"mux\", \"parity\"], [6, 11, 27, 4, 19])\n\nc.onPick = { pick ->\n    println(\"clicked ${pick.category} = ${pick.y} (index ${pick.index})\")\n    println(\"  the hit itself: ${pick.hit.kind} at ${pick.hit.position}\")\n}\n\nshowChart(c)\n\n/*\nSet `pickable = false` for a figure whose points are scenery rather than data: it is\none registered callback and one <g> per datum, which a ten-thousand-point scatter\ndoes not want.\n*/\nprintln(\"marks: \" + c.marks)\n"}}}] "persistentProperties" ["frame" "name" "code" "code_language" "contents_language" "default_property" "file_proxy_mode" "file_proxy_path" "file_proxy_original_path" "file_proxy_mime" "filename" "preamble" "introduction" "attic" "notes" "geometryReviews" "geometryAppearance" "fragment" "stackSL" "rectSL" "msl" "vertex" "vertex_fragment" "task" "mesh" "raygen" "miss" "closesthit" "anyhit" "intersection" "callable" "fragment_s" "vertex_s" "task_s" "mesh_s" "raygen_s" "miss_s" "closesthit_s" "anyhit_s" "intersection_s" "callable_s" "children" "parents" "compute" "compute_s" "customization" "source_id" "templateId" "_tombstone" "isRemoteJSRoot" "isPythonRoot" ".*_editorState" "metadata" "nested" "nestedIn" "playlist" "pauseList" "bookmarks" "drawing" "panes" "slideShow" "autorun" "layers" "layerFrames" "_controlGroupValues" "keyframeData" "keyframeInterpolants" "ai4_auto_name" "ai4_auto_name_signature" "ai4_chat_history" "ai4_chat_running" "ai4_chat_suspension" "ai4_session" "fileManagerAccessHistory" "kitRole"]}